WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Film Courage: You mentioned something earlier about
creating story worlds? Where did that

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.429
come from? Where did that occur to you?
Most people probably don't think again

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:13.530
in those terms? Where is this
coming from the story world idea?

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:22.949
Adam Davis, Writer/Director: Sure well Will and I we had this idea
for the sci-fi series so we were pretty

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:29.849
interested in how do we make a world a
sci-fi world that doesn't exist yet you

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:34.710
know like because there have been great
iconic sci-fi worlds created and we

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:40.890
wanted to do something sort of on that
level but we had to figure out like how

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:47.399
to build a world which is a super hard
and fascinating thing to do you know so

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:52.500
it's like if if there's this technology
that we create like how does that affect

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.170
the world you know let's let's try to
build that up you know so we started

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:05.010
thinking about you know the macro stuff
like how does politics work how does

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:10.740
religion work how are people how does
this technology affect them and you just

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.619
sort of go out in these branches and try
to nail down this world because you have

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:21.780
to be super specific you know because
you need to know how everything operates

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:28.409
you know how people would react in this
world do they speak differently did this

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:35.040
piece of technology alter it in that way
you know and in doing all of that work

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.159
and we spent I would say a solid six
months just building the world before we

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:46.680
even wrote the story you know we had a
general idea of what we wanted the story

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:52.350
to be you know going back to like what
we're trying to say but we had to really

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:58.079
flesh out this world and figure out what
would work and what didn't work and how

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:02.520
it was different from other things and
and how it had touchpoints of

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.110
similarities so it's not so alien that
people you know are sort of thrown by

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:12.720
can't get into it but yeah full at least
six months trying to

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.130
create this thing in order to just start
thinking about the story and the

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.300
characters that would really populate it
do you ever listen to some of the

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.810
science fiction authors or or
screenwriters on how they've like a

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:29.160
Philip K Dick or whatever on how they
would create worlds or just the

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.420
headspace they were in I realized some
of it was maybe chemically induced but

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.320
some but you know even like Ursula K Le
Guin just she was a mother of four and

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.440
then she's coming up with these amazing
worlds I don't know how she's doing it

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.820
and then seems incredibly saying in
these interviews you know and and do you

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.430
ever listen to just these people in
their process you know I haven't really

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:57.870
it was sort of the thing where we just
made what we thought would be a good

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:03.030
process ourselves like let's just sort
of blue-sky this and it was just a

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:09.300
series of long conversations and you
know just figuring out what we thought

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.380
would work and what sort of bumped
against what could work maybe it's best

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.430
you didn't because then you might try to
emulate that style and then and then

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.030
it's too cliche right and then it's like
oh I see what they were going for they

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:25.769
were trying to do you know scanner dark
there so you know have you carried that

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:29.010
world-building over into other projects
or it's just for this one that you're

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:36.150
working on specifically now well I think
maybe not the world building itself but

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:44.940
how we created sort of one a working
relationship and and how we thought of

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:50.269
ideas and stories I think that's what we
really took for it you know for example

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:55.700
I didn't need to create this whole like
big world document for broken ceiling

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:00.810
because it's you know set in now in an
industry that I know that I'm you know

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:06.750
aware of so for something like that I
didn't need to do you know if something

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:11.160
set in in present day or in the world
you don't necessarily have to do that

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:17.970
however for this period piece pilot
about miners that we wrote we did have

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:23.400
to create a world there too because you
know it's set in the 1920s which we

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.450
obviously didn't live through and
I was like well do we want to make a

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:31.680
heightened version of that do we want to
you know stick straight to the

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.020
historical accuracy so that was
something that was sort of a follow-up

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.060
project to that we're you know even
though it's it's a period piece

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.230
we still had to sort of create that
world like how these characters wouldn't

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:51.150
live so it really depends on on the
story and where it is time and I assume

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:57.030
that if I was gonna make something you
know in present day there was something

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.750
that I didn't even know about you know
sit in another country or whatever I had

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:05.370
I would have to do that same sort of
world building as well you know based on

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:09.930
research of course do you ever put a
time limit on your research because I

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:16.740
would imagine it would become extremely
fun sure yeah I mean um I'm not as much

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.930
as I've had to do research it's not my
favorite part I really want to get to

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:29.700
the writing and the story breaking and
the character stuff but but yeah there

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:34.560
there is a thing and this goes not only
for world building research but

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.590
character research there is such a thing
as too much research you know like for

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:47.610
example you don't need to necessarily
know what a character was doing when

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:52.650
they were 8 years old right you may not
need to create that much of a character

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:58.920
background or history if this character
is in their 30s you know and it's said

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:05.340
over the course of a week like how much
is that really gonna inform you or is it

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.570
the type of thing where you're gonna
want to find a way to shoehorn in that

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:14.640
memory from you know when they were
eight years old into the scene because

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.810
you did the work so I think it's it's a
very interesting sort of dichotomy of

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:26.370
how much is too much
research what you need don't over

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:34.040
research and and make sure that you're
actually getting those pages are written

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:41.699
you

